        "title": "UNESCO world heritage site - one of the most visited Shiva temples",
        "text": "The temple was not too crowded, which made the experience pleasant. The darshan (idol sighting) lasted only a few seconds. That was not disappointing as it was more than what most of us get at teh busy temples in India. Did it stand out as a world heritage site? Nope. Durbar square had better views and more intricate architecture. Did it stand out as one of the most sacred Shiva temples? I dont know, but the buzz of people, the stores selling rudraksha(sacred seed) garlands all along the pavement gave it its own charm. The graveyard which is situated just behind the temple was a bit unpleasant. I was not comfortable watching people burn their dead. Since Shiva is the known as the protector of the Graveyard, its location is apt. Its just good good to know what to expect before you visit. Its a religious Hindu's dream to visit Pashupathinath. I think it deserves one visit. People practising other religions are not allowed into the main temple. Leather bags and cameras are not allowed.",
